# ── Chapter 11 fidelity pins: "Where People Are Put" ─────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/11-where-people-are-put.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# The "alarm that does not work" section pins a DEFECT. err(_, Placement) fires
# on Ruk and Lalo, both correctly placed, and on nobody misplaced. When the rule
# is repaired to look at where someone was placed rather than whether they have a
# home, err(Ruk) and err(Lalo) both flip FALSE and that section is rewritten.

:expect-pins 41

# Hano — meant it, and that alone is not enough. These two pins belong next to
# each other: with no dimension recorded at all, severe(Hano) would be FALSE for
# want of anything to test, and the threshold would be unguarded.
? attack(Hano, Ivo).
# => TRUE
? severe(Hano).
# => FALSE

# "Hano has no family on record either." — the input the chapter warns about at
# its opening, pinned on the man the warning is about: one written word here and
# Hano leaves his house for a facility (chapter 1 runs that exact write).
? family(Hano).
# => FALSE
? fit(Hano, Homestay).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Hano).
# => TRUE

# Ruk — severe, non-domestic: severity blocks eligibility, high security.
# Severity here is intent plus a second person harmed, and the second person is
# an ordinary injury entry, not a count of anything.
? attack(Ruk, Opal).
# => TRUE
? injure(Ruk, Pax).
# => TRUE
? severe(Ruk).
# => TRUE
? family(Ruk).
# => FALSE
? fit(Ruk, Homestay).
# => FALSE
? building(HighSec, Ruk).
# => TRUE

# Nando — domestic, not severe: low security. The family entry does not merely
# route Nando somewhere; it closes the home-confinement door first, and that
# half was pinned nowhere until now.
? family(Nando).
# => TRUE
? fit(Nando, Homestay).
# => FALSE
? severe(Nando).
# => FALSE
? building(LowSec, Nando).
# => TRUE

# Lalo — domestic AND severe: high security. Severity by the other road,
# intent and cruelty on a single act. The family fact is what makes the case
# domestic, and it went unpinned while the chapter's walk leaned on it.
? family(Lalo).
# => TRUE
? attack(Lalo, Mina).
# => TRUE
? cruel(Lalo, Mina).
# => TRUE
? severe(Lalo).
# => TRUE
? building(HighSec, Lalo).
# => TRUE

# Don — cruelty plus a second person harmed. Don has no home and no family, so
# before severity derived he had no placement at all; he is the reason the third
# route is exercised rather than decorative.
? severe(Don).
# => TRUE
? home(Don).
# => FALSE
? building(HighSec, Don).
# => TRUE

# THE BROKEN ALARM. Both flagged; both correctly placed. Hano, correctly
# home-confined, is not flagged — so the marker tracks "has a home and is not
# homestay-eligible", which is the ordinary condition of a severe offender.
:defect "keying err/2 on where somebody was PUT, not on having a home"
? err(Ruk, Placement).
# => TRUE
:defect "keying err/2 on where somebody was PUT, not on having a home"
? err(Lalo, Placement).
# => TRUE
? err(Hano, Placement).
# => FALSE

# Nando has no home fact, so the marker cannot reach him either way
? home(Nando).
# => FALSE

# THE CASE NOBODY WROTE A RULE FOR. Not severe, no family, no home: eligibility
# derives and, until v0.8, nothing else did. The two FALSE building queries stay
# load-bearing — "the missing thing is not a destination" is the chapter's whole
# argument, and if a facility rule ever reaches these two the section is wrong.
? fit(Kel, Homestay).
# => TRUE
? home(Kel).
# => FALSE
? building(HighSec, Kel).
# => FALSE
? building(LowSec, Kel).
# => FALSE
? fit(Adam, Homestay).
# => TRUE

# [v0.8] "Kel and Adam are housed." Shelter now follows from being confined with
# nowhere to be confined — NOT from a placement. These two were FALSE and the
# chapter's closing section turned on their being FALSE; it now turns on their
# being TRUE, so they are the pins that say which version of the chapter is in
# print.
? dwell(Kel).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Adam).
# => TRUE

# "Every convicted person in this design now has shelter ... the routes are
#  rules, and between them they leave no combination of the three facts
#  uncovered." The claim is universal over prisoners, so it is pinned across
#  the cast rather than on the two new cases — one witness per route.
? dwell(Hano).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Ruk).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Nando).
# => TRUE
? dwell(Don).
# => TRUE

# ...and it reaches nobody who is not confined. Bela is the test: voided,
# unrecognised, and not a prisoner.
? dwell(Bela).
# => FALSE

# "Nothing anywhere says Nando's victim was family; the rule never asked."
? family(Nando).
# => TRUE
? family(Opal).
# => FALSE
